Parity reproduced exactly from the web source (cross-platform value parity, not a "corrected" version):
  decoded = vin.length < 11 ? null : {
    mfr   : VIN_MANUFACTURERS[upper.slice(0,3)] ?? t('Unknown'),
    model : VIN_MODELS[upper[3]]  ?? t('Unknown'),
    drive : VIN_DRIVE[upper[7]]   ?? t('Unknown'),
    year  : VIN_YEAR[upper[9]]    ?? t('Unknown'),
    plant : VIN_PLANT[upper[10]]  ?? t('Unknown'),
    serial: upper.slice(11) }
  upper = vin.toUpperCase(); grid order = mfr, model, drive, year, plant, serial.
  Tables ported verbatim: 4 manufacturers, 4 models, 10 drive, 10 model-year, 6 plant codes.

i18n keys extracted from the web source (table "VinDecoder"): "Vin Decoder", "Vin Decoder Desc", "Vin",
"Unknown", and the six dynamic "devtools.utils.vin_<mfr|model|drive|year|plant|serial>" grid labels.
The web en.json carries "Vin" and "Unknown" verbatim, but has NO entry for "Vin Decoder" / "Vin Decoder
Desc" and NO "devtools.utils.vin_*" keys (the devtools.utils block holds only base64/base64Desc/
computeSha256), so i18next renders those keys AS-IS. The native fallbacks reproduce that exactly (parity;
identical to how predecessor 0011 Base64Tool ships the ugly en.json value "Base64Desc" verbatim).
Native-only chrome adds the too-short hint. The view holds no bare English literals (all via VinDecoderStrings).

States: this is a pure synchronous compute tool — the web source has NO data fetching (no useQuery), so
loading / error / stale / offline are N/A by parity (no async source exists to be in those states). The
web source's two real branches are reproduced and tested: a decodable VIN (>= 11 chars) → the field grid;
a too-short VIN → the grid is hidden (native renders a friendly hint, never a blank box). Within the
content branch, unmapped segments render the localized "Unknown" (web `?? t('Unknown')`), and an 11-char
VIN's empty serial renders an em dash so no tile is ever blank — both reproduced + tested.

